From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 18:26:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: the four refusal sites the spin
 rule did not reach
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0015 put SealedHangOnAllocationFailure() first in each of the
seven places the runtime gives up on memory, and said so in its
message: "every MmapOrDie variant funnels through ReportMmapFailureAndDie",
plus the ENOMEM-tolerating shadow paths and the internal allocator.  A
downstream sweep -- over the pinned compiler-rt and
this series, each candidate handed to an independent refuter and dropped
unless confirmed, 44 examined, 16 kept -- found that count covers 12 of
the 16, and that the way the four were missed is itself the method for not
missing them.  Sort every mmap wrapper by reading its body:

  (A) dies unconditionally: MmapOrDie, MmapFixedOrDie, MmapNoReserveOrDie,
      and the OnFatalError variants on anything but ENOMEM.  All funnel
      through ReportMmapFailureAndDie.  Covered by 0015.
  (B) tolerates ENOMEM and returns null or false: MmapFixedNoReserve,
      MmapFixedSuperNoReserve, MmapOrDieOnFatalError,
      MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError, MmapAlignedOrDieOnFatalError.  The
      answer lives at each CALL SITE, and 0015 only had the shadow's.
  (C) checks nothing and returns internal_mmap's raw -errno: MmapNoAccess
      and MmapFixedNoAccess.  Covered only where the caller tests the
      value before using it; ProtectGap does, and one caller did not.

The four, in the order they matter:

1. ReservedAddressRange::Init (sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp), class (C).
   Not a death.  It handed MmapNoAccess's -errno back as the base, and
   InitAligned's fixup, start += align - (start & (align - 1)), takes
   every -errno in -1..-4095 to exactly 0 (the sweep ran all 4095), so a
   refused primary-allocator reservation came up as an arena based at
   address 0, passed CHECK_NE(NonConstSpaceBeg, ~0), and ran until the
   first allocation poisoned shadow for an address inside the gap.  The
   secondary's chunk array uses the same Init and its CHECK(p) passes on
   -ENOMEM directly.  The test is in Init, before the value is used, so it
   covers both callers and InitAligned's fixup never sees an error.
   Unsealed behaviour is unchanged.
2. QuarantineCallback::Allocate (asan_allocator.cpp), class (B), on the
   first free().  It calls ReportOutOfMemory unconditionally -- it does
   not consult AllocatorMayReturnNull(), unlike the user-facing Allocate
   -- so allocator_may_return_null does not save it, and the ENOMEM
   behind its null was tolerated in MmapFixedImpl where no die-helper saw
   it.  This is the runtime's own allocation, made inside a call the
   plain build makes without allocating, so the rule applies: spin.
3. PoisonShadow (asan_poisoning.cpp): the CHECK where site 1 used to die.
   Init now stops it at the source; this holds the CHECK to the same rule
   at the place the corruption surfaced, so that no other route to an
   address outside the application ranges becomes a report instead.
4. SizeClassAllocator64::ReturnToAllocator (primary64.h), class (B):
   EnsureFreeArraySpace maps through MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError and its
   ENOMEM is answered at this call site with Report and Die.  Needs 65536
   frees in one size class, so not a start-up site; converted under
   0015's rule that the seal holds for the life of the process.

The non-mmap candidates -- /proc reads, getrlimit, madvise, /dev/urandom, the
stack depot, gettid -- came back empty as shipped: the seal already
removed those calls.  Same exception as 0015: a failure while a report is
in progress aborts.

The suite gains sealed_arena_refused, whose wrapper answers EPERM to the
one PROT_NONE mmap of 4GiB or more at no fixed address that a sealed
start-up makes; before this patch the sealed build dies through site 3
(measured on x86_64 with strace injecting EPERM on mmap #6: region info
mapped at 0x40000000000 = 0 + the arena size, then the CHECK, gettid, two
writes, exit_group(1)), and after it the trace ends at the refused mmap and
the build is still running at the timeout with nothing written, the
verdict sealed_alloc_refused is held to.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_allocator.cpp               | 10 +++++++++-
 compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_poisoning.cpp               |  8 ++++++++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator.h        |  1 +
 .../sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator_primary64.h  |  5 +++++
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp  | 11 +++++++++++
 5 files changed, 34 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_allocator.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_allocator.cpp
index 3a55c2af6..6b92cdc3b 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_allocator.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_allocator.cpp
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_internal_defs.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_list.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_quarantine.h"
+#include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_stackdepot.h"
 
 namespace __asan {
@@ -265,8 +266,15 @@ struct QuarantineCallback {
   void *Allocate(uptr size) const {
     void *res = get_allocator().Allocate(cache_, size, 1);
     // TODO(alekseys): Consider making quarantine OOM-friendly.
-    if (UNLIKELY(!res))
+    // This is the runtime's own allocation, made inside free(), and it
+    // reports unconditionally -- allocator_may_return_null is not consulted,
+    // unlike the user-facing path in Allocate().  The ENOMEM behind a null
+    // here was tolerated by MmapFixedImpl, so no die-helper saw it: a sealed
+    // binary stops at this call site.  See SealedHangOnAllocationFailure.
+    if (UNLIKELY(!res)) {
+      SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
       ReportOutOfMemory(size, stack_);
+    }
     return res;
   }
 
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_poisoning.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_poisoning.cpp
index 762670632..ca5894ef8 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_poisoning.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_poisoning.cpp
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_flags.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_interface_internal.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_libc.h"
+#include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h"
 
 namespace __asan {
 
@@ -36,6 +37,13 @@ bool CanPoisonMemory() {
 void PoisonShadow(uptr addr, uptr size, u8 value) {
   if (value && !CanPoisonMemory()) return;
   CHECK(AddrIsAlignedByGranularity(addr));
+  // An address outside the application ranges is what a laundered
+  // allocation failure looks like from here (an arena based at 0 poisons
+  // into the shadow gap).  ReservedAddressRange::Init now stops that at its
+  // source; this is the same rule at the place the CHECK would have fired,
+  // so that no other route to it becomes a report.
+  if (UNLIKELY(!AddrIsInMem(addr)))
+    SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
   CHECK(AddrIsInMem(addr));
   CHECK(AddrIsAlignedByGranularity(addr + size));
   CHECK(AddrIsInMem(addr + size - ASAN_SHADOW_GRANULARITY));
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator.h
index 0b28f86d1..fd9f86873 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator.h
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_local_address_space_view.h"
 #include "sanitizer_mutex.h"
 #include "sanitizer_procmaps.h"
+#include "sanitizer_sealed.h"
 #include "sanitizer_type_traits.h"
 
 namespace __sanitizer {
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator_primary64.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator_primary64.h
index 51ac1b6ae..bca255449 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator_primary64.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_allocator_primary64.h
@@ -185,6 +185,11 @@ class SizeClassAllocator64 {
     // recoverable.
     if (UNLIKELY(!EnsureFreeArraySpace(region, region_beg,
                                        new_num_freed_chunks))) {
+      // EnsureFreeArraySpace maps through MmapFixedOrDieOnFatalError, which
+      // tolerates ENOMEM and answers it here, so this is the call site the
+      // sealed rule applies to; any other mmap error already stopped inside
+      // the die-helper.  See SealedHangOnAllocationFailure.
+      SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
       Report(
           "FATAL: Internal error: %s's allocator exhausted the free list "
           "space for size class %zu (%zu bytes).\n",
diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
index 43fe0c871..975d7c0ee 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp
@@ -422,6 +422,17 @@ bool MmapFixedSuperNoReserve(uptr fixed_addr, uptr size, const char *name) {
 uptr ReservedAddressRange::Init(uptr size, const char *name, uptr fixed_addr) {
   base_ = fixed_addr ? MmapFixedNoAccess(fixed_addr, size, name)
                      : MmapNoAccess(size);
+  // MmapNoAccess and MmapFixedNoAccess are the two mmap wrappers that check
+  // nothing: they hand back internal_mmap's raw -errno, and this is the only
+  // caller that then treats the value as an address.  InitAligned's fixup,
+  // start += align - (start & (align - 1)), takes every -errno in -1..-4095
+  // to exactly 0, so a refused primary-allocator reservation would become an
+  // arena based at address 0 and pass the CHECK_NE(..., ~0) downstream; the
+  // secondary's chunk array passes its CHECK(p) on the raw -ENOMEM directly.
+  // A sealed binary stops here instead, before the value is used.  Unsealed
+  // behaviour is unchanged.  See SealedHangOnAllocationFailure.
+  if (UNLIKELY(internal_iserror(reinterpret_cast<uptr>(base_))))
+    SealedHangOnAllocationFailure();
   size_ = size;
   name_ = name;
   (void)os_handle_;  // unsupported
